From 0a3b208b9296e6959c4016dd5a04ed58cd5c5423 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andre Watson <dre@ligandal.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 21:51:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/32] feat(spec): per-tier adaptive acceptance counters,
 width-correct accept rate

Under adaptive speculation the scheduler's reported accept_rate was a ratio between
numbers gathered at different widths: num_correct_drafts accumulates over the whole
log interval, while draft_per_round was read from the CURRENTLY active config at the
end of it. A switch inside the interval therefore divided one width's accepted drafts
by another width's capacity. The switch is applied in on_verify_complete_cpu, which
runs before update_spec_metrics on the same batch, so the active config is not even
the width that produced the batch being recorded.

Two changes:

1. metrics_reporter accumulates the draft CAPACITY per batch. The caller passes the
   width from batch.spec_info.draft_token_num -- the batch's own verify input, which
   is swap-proof. The old active-config denominator remains as the fallback for any
   path that does not report a width, where it is exact because the width is fixed.

2. AdaptiveSpeculativeParams keeps per-(bs slot, steps) counters, attributed to the
   pre-update width that actually produced the drafts, and exposes tier_stats() /
   tier_stats_line(). Set "tier_stats_log_interval" in the adaptive config JSON to
   have them logged periodically. Without this there is no way to read
   accept-length-by-tier off a running server: the aggregate accept_len mixes tiers,
   and the "Adaptive spec params updated" lines only say when a switch happened.

Tests: test/registered/unit/speculative/test_adaptive_wide.py, 22 cases, CPU only,
driving the production policy and the production QSA ring sizing (no re-implementations).
Covers the ring-serves-every-tier coupling, the per-state CUDA-graph pruning invariant
(every graph batch size has its routed tier captured) and its VRAM budget, the derived
hysteresis (switches on the right side of the modelled break-even, settles rather than
oscillating, and the bs>=4 slots never widen past the static width), and the per-tier
counters. Runs under pytest and, with a shim, as a plain script in a venv without pytest.
